(
  $q$how-to-wear-a-cotton-saree$q$,
  $q$How to Wear a Cotton Saree: Step-by-Step Draping Guide for Beginners$q$,
  $q$Learn how to drape a cotton saree neatly in 7 easy steps, from pleats and pallu to starching tips, so it stays crisp and comfortable all day long.$q$,
  ARRAY[
    $q$how to wear a cotton saree$q$,
    $q$cotton saree draping$q$,
    $q$saree draping for beginners$q$,
    $q$cotton saree for women$q$
  ]::text[],
  $q$https://images.pexels.com/photos/1191349/pexels-photo-1191349.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200&h=630&fit=crop$q$,
  ARRAY[
    $q$A cotton saree is the easiest six yards to live in. It breathes, it does not slip, and it holds a pleat far better than silk or georgette. Yet many first-time wearers struggle because cotton behaves differently: it is crisp, it has body, and it rewards a little preparation. This guide walks you through a clean, classic drape that works for college, office or a family lunch.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Before you start: what you need}}$q$,
    $q$Wear a fitted blouse and a cotton petticoat in a shade close to your saree. Choose a petticoat that sits snugly at the waist, because the drape is only as stable as the base it is tucked into. Wear the footwear you plan to use, since the hem should just skim the floor. Keep about six safety pins ready, and iron the saree lightly if it feels creased.$q$,
    $q${{h3:Starch or no starch?}}$q$,
    $q$Soft [Mulmul Sarees](categoryslug:mulmul-saree) look best with no starch at all, so the fabric falls in gentle waves. Crisp [Handloom Sarees](categoryslug:handloom-saree) and everyday weaves look sharper with light starch. If you are a beginner, a little body helps the pleats stay in place, so a spray of light starch before ironing is a good middle path.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Step-by-step: the classic Nivi drape}}$q$,
    $q${{h3:Step 1: Tuck the plain end}}$q$,
    $q$Hold the saree with the plain end (the end without the heavy pallu) in your right hand. Tuck it into the petticoat at your navel and start wrapping to the right. Keep the lower edge just above the floor all the way round, then bring the end back to the starting point. This first wrap decides how straight the whole saree looks.$q$,
    $q${{h3:Step 2: Make the pleats}}$q$,
    $q$Gather about one arm's length of fabric and fold it into five to seven pleats, each roughly the width of your palm. Cotton holds a pleat well, so run your fingers down the folds to line them up, then hold them together with the borders facing the same way. Uneven pleats are the most common beginner mistake, so slow down here.$q$,
    $q${{h3:Step 3: Tuck the pleats in}}$q$,
    $q$Tuck the pleats into the petticoat just left of the navel, with the pleats opening towards the left. They should fall straight down to your ankles. Give them a gentle tug to check they are firm. If the cotton feels slippery after washing, a single pin inside the waist keeps them from sliding.$q$,
    $q${{h3:Step 4: Drape the pallu}}$q$,
    $q$Carry the remaining fabric across your back and bring it over your left shoulder. The pallu should reach somewhere between your knee and mid-calf. Pin it to the blouse at the shoulder, using a pin that grips only the blouse and a small fold of the saree, so the fabric does not pucker.$q$,
    $q${{h3:Step 5: Set the pallu pleats}}$q$,
    $q$For an office or daily look, pleat the pallu neatly across the shoulder and pin it in three places. For a relaxed look, leave it open and let the border show. Heavier cotton and woven borders look best when the pallu is allowed to fall in a clean vertical line.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Three drapes that suit cotton}}$q$,
    $q$Besides the Nivi drape, cotton works beautifully in the Bengali style with a wide open pallu, the Gujarati seedha pallu worn from the back to the front, and a simple half-pleated drape that is quick to wear on busy mornings. Plain solid colours such as those in our [Plain Sarees](categoryslug:plain-saree) range show the drape clearly, which makes them ideal for learning.$q$,
    $q${{product:plain-cotton-silk-sarees}}$q$,
    $q${{h2:Common mistakes and how to fix them}}$q$,
    $q$If the saree feels too short, the first wrap was too tight or the pleats were too deep, so unpin and start again with slightly narrower pleats. If the pallu keeps slipping, move the pin closer to the blouse seam and pin through the blouse stitching. If the hem drags, check your footwear height before you re-drape. And if the fabric looks bulky at the waist, use a petticoat made of thinner cotton.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Choosing the right cotton saree to practise on}}$q$,
    $q$If you are learning, start with a medium-weight cotton saree of about 5.5 metres with a simple border. Very thin fabrics slip, and very heavy weaves are bulky to pleat. A saree with a clear contrast border also helps, because you can see at a glance whether your pleats and hem line up. Printed sarees hide small mistakes, while solid colours show every fold, so pick whichever suits your confidence level.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Quick styling ideas once you have the basics}}$q$,
    $q$Once the drape feels natural, experiment a little. Try a belt over the saree for a modern look, a half-sleeve jacket-style blouse for work, or a crisp white blouse with a coloured saree for summer. Pair cotton with oxidised silver jewellery for a relaxed festive feel, or with simple pearl studs for a quiet, polished look. A well-worn cotton saree rarely needs more than one statement piece.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Make it last all day}}$q$,
    $q$Cotton wrinkles naturally, which is part of its charm, so do not worry about a few soft creases by evening. Carry two spare pins, sit with your pleats gathered gently in your hand, and avoid sitting directly on the pallu. Browse our full collection of [Cotton Sarees for Women](categoryslug:cotton-saree) when you are ready to practise on a new drape, and read our [cotton saree care guide](blog:cotton-saree-care-guide) to keep the fabric fresh wash after wash.$q$
  ]::text[],
  $q$[{"question":"How many pleats should a cotton saree have?","answer":"Five to seven pleats of about a palm's width each is ideal. Cotton is firm enough that fewer, wider pleats look neat and are easy to walk in."},{"question":"Do I need starch to drape a cotton saree?","answer":"Not always. Soft mulmul cotton drapes better without starch, while crisper handloom cotton looks sharper with light starch. Beginners often find a little starch makes pleats easier to hold."},{"question":"Which petticoat is best under a cotton saree?","answer":"A fitted cotton petticoat in a shade close to the saree. It grips the pleats better than satin and keeps the saree comfortable in hot weather."},{"question":"How long does it take to wear a saree?","answer":"Most beginners take 10 to 15 minutes. With a few weeks of practice, you can drape a cotton saree in under five minutes."}]$q$::jsonb,
  5,
