  $q$handloom-vs-mill-cotton-saree$q$,
  $q$Handloom vs Mill Cotton Saree: Differences, Price and How to Choose$q$,
  $q$Handloom or mill-made? Compare feel, weave, durability and price, and learn simple tests to tell them apart before you buy your next cotton saree.$q$,
  ARRAY[
    $q$handloom vs mill cotton saree$q$,
    $q$handloom saree$q$,
    $q$how to identify handloom saree$q$,
    $q$cotton saree buying guide$q$
  ]::text[],
  $q$https://images.pexels.com/photos/2058197/pexels-photo-2058197.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200&h=630&fit=crop$q$,
  ARRAY[
    $q$When you shop for a cotton saree, you will often see two labels: handloom and mill-made, sometimes called powerloom. Both are cotton, both can be beautiful, and both have a place in a wardrobe. The difference lies in how the cloth is woven, and that affects how the saree looks, feels, lasts and is priced. This guide explains the practical differences so you can choose with confidence.$q$,
    $q${{h2:How each saree is made}}$q$,
    $q${{h3:Handloom cotton}}$q$,
    $q$A handloom saree is woven by a weaver working on a hand-operated loom. The weaver controls the tension of the threads and often builds the border and pallu design by hand. Because of this, a single saree can take anywhere from a day to several days to complete, depending on the design. Regions such as Bengal, Odisha, Andhra and Tamil Nadu each have their own signature handloom styles.$q$,
    $q${{h3:Mill or powerloom cotton}}$q$,
    $q$A mill saree is woven on an electric machine loom. The machine produces long lengths of uniform fabric quickly, then the cloth is cut and finished into sarees. This keeps cost lower and makes the weave very consistent, which is why mill cotton is popular for daily wear and uniforms.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Handloom vs mill cotton at a glance}}$q$,
    $q$Feel: handloom cotton is usually softer after a few washes and has a slightly irregular, natural texture. Mill cotton is smooth and even from day one. Weave: tiny variations in the yarn are normal and desirable in handloom. In mill cotton, every thread looks identical. Design: handloom motifs are woven into the cloth, so the pattern looks the same on both sides, while printed mill designs often look faded on the reverse.$q$,
    $q$Price: handloom sarees cost more because of the time and skill involved. Durability: both last for years with proper care, though handloom cotton often becomes more comfortable with age. Breathability: both are excellent in hot weather, and handloom is often a little airier because of its open weave.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Simple tests to tell them apart}}$q$,
    $q$Check the reverse side. In handloom you will usually see small loose threads and slight irregularities where the weaver changed the design. In mill cloth the back is very neat or shows floating threads cut by a machine. Look at the selvedge too, as hand-woven edges are slightly uneven. Finally, hold the saree to the light: handloom cotton shows small variations in thickness, while mill cotton looks uniform.$q$,
    $q$Be careful with tests that sound too good to be true, such as burning a thread. These tests only tell you the fibre, not whether the saree is handloom, so rely on the weave, the finish and a trustworthy seller instead.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Which one should you buy?}}$q$,
    $q$Choose handloom when you want something with character, a saree for festivals, or a piece you will wear for years. Our [Handloom Sarees](categoryslug:handloom-saree) range includes soft hand-woven cotton in classic colours with contrast pallus.$q$,
    $q${{product:magenta-cotton-handloom-sarees-with-mustard-pallu}}$q$,
    $q$Choose mill cotton when you need practical everyday sarees at a lower price, particularly for work or travel, or when you want crisp [Plain Sarees](categoryslug:plain-saree) in many colours. If you are building a wardrobe on a budget, our [Sarees Under 500](categoryslug:under-499-saree) and [Sarees Under 1000](tag:saree-under-1000) pages are a good place to start.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Real-life comfort: what you will notice when you wear them}}$q$,
    $q$On a hot afternoon, the difference between the two becomes obvious. Handloom cotton often feels airy and slightly textured against the skin, and it tends to soften further with every wash. Mill cotton feels smooth and even, and it is usually quicker to iron because the weave is flat and regular. Handloom sarees can feel a little heavier to carry in the first few wears but relax beautifully once washed. Mill sarees are lighter on the budget and on the arm, which is why many working women keep a stack of them for weekday use.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Questions to ask before you buy a handloom saree}}$q$,
    $q$Ask where the saree was woven and what the yarn is, whether it is pure cotton or a blend. Ask whether the colours are fast and how the seller recommends washing it. A genuine seller will answer these questions plainly. Check for small knots and slight irregularities, which are normal in hand weaving, but reject a saree with large holes, broken threads or patchy colour. Finally, look at the return policy, so you can exchange the saree if the colour looks different in daylight.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Mixing both in your wardrobe}}$q$,
    $q$Most saree lovers do not pick one over the other. A sensible mix is two or three handloom sarees for occasions and four or five easy-care cotton sarees for weekdays. Soft [Mulmul Sarees](categoryslug:mulmul-saree) are excellent for summer, while a heavier handloom with a woven border suits winter lunches and festive visits.$q$,
    $q${{h2:How to care for both}}$q$,
    $q$Wash new handloom cotton separately for the first two or three washes in cold water, because natural dyes can release colour. Mill cotton is more forgiving but still prefers mild detergent. Dry both in shade to protect the colour. Our [cotton saree care guide](blog:cotton-saree-care-guide) covers washing, starching and storage in detail.$q$
  ]::text[],
  $q$[{"question":"Is handloom cotton better than mill cotton?","answer":"Neither is universally better. Handloom offers craftsmanship and a softer, more individual feel, while mill cotton is consistent, affordable and practical for daily wear."},{"question":"Why are handloom sarees more expensive?","answer":"They are woven by hand, which takes more time and skill, and designs are often built into the weave rather than printed on afterwards."},{"question":"Can I wash a handloom saree at home?","answer":"Yes, in most cases. Use cold water and a mild detergent, wash it separately at first, and dry it in the shade. Check the label if the saree has zari or special dyes."},{"question":"How can I quickly check if a saree is handloom?","answer":"Look at the reverse side and the edges. Small irregularities, uneven selvedges and patterns that look identical on both sides usually point to hand weaving."}]$q$::jsonb,
