  $q$cotton-saree-care-guide$q$,
  $q$Cotton Saree Care Guide: Washing, Starching, Ironing and Storage$q$,
  $q$Keep your cotton sarees soft, bright and crisp for years. Our practical guide covers washing, starching, ironing, stain removal and storage in humid weather.$q$,
  ARRAY[
    $q$cotton saree care$q$,
    $q$how to wash cotton saree$q$,
    $q$how to starch cotton saree$q$,
    $q$cotton saree maintenance$q$
  ]::text[],
  $q$https://images.pexels.com/photos/8839887/pexels-photo-8839887.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200&h=630&fit=crop$q$,
  ARRAY[
    $q$Cotton is one of the most forgiving saree fabrics, but it still pays to treat it well. A little care keeps the colours bright, the fabric soft and the borders crisp for years. This guide covers everything from the first wash to long-term storage, in simple steps you can follow at home.$q$,
    $q${{h2:The first wash}}$q$,
    $q$New cotton sarees, especially dark and bold shades, can release extra dye in the first few washes. Wash a new saree separately in cold water, and never soak it for hours. A short soak of ten to fifteen minutes is enough. Adding a handful of rock salt to the first rinse is a traditional way to help set colour, though a separate wash is the safest habit.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Regular washing}}$q$,
    $q${{h3:Hand wash or machine wash?}}$q$,
    $q$Hand washing is gentlest and is the best choice for handloom and delicate borders. If you use a machine, choose a gentle cycle, place the saree in a mesh bag and keep the spin low. Use a mild liquid detergent, because harsh powders can fade colour and weaken threads over time.$q$,
    $q${{h3:How often should you wash?}}$q$,
    $q$Cotton sarees worn in hot weather should be washed after every wear or two, since sweat can weaken fibres. In cooler months, you can air them out and wash after three or four wears.$q$,
    $q${{h3:Rinsing and drying}}$q$,
    $q$Rinse until the water runs clear, then squeeze gently without wringing or twisting. Dry in shade rather than direct sun, because strong sunlight fades colour, especially on dark and natural-dyed sarees. Hang the saree evenly across a line so the weight is spread, and flip it halfway so the borders dry properly.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Starching for a crisp drape}}$q$,
    $q$Starch gives cotton a neat, structured look that holds pleats beautifully. Mix a small spoonful of rice or arrowroot starch in warm water, let it cool, and dip the saree for a minute. Squeeze lightly and dry in shade. For a lighter option, a commercial starch spray applied just before ironing works well. Remember that soft [Mulmul Sarees](categoryslug:mulmul-saree) look best with little or no starch, while crisper [Handloom Sarees](categoryslug:handloom-saree) can take more.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Ironing the right way}}$q$,
    $q$Iron cotton while it is slightly damp, using a medium-to-high setting. Start with the borders and pallu, then move to the body, ironing on the reverse side to protect woven motifs. If the saree has zari or heavy thread work, use a thin cotton cloth between the iron and the fabric. Fold it immediately after ironing, in the same way you plan to store it.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Removing common stains}}$q$,
    $q$Act quickly. For oil, sprinkle baking soda or a little talcum powder on the spot for ten minutes, brush it off, then wash with mild detergent. For sweat marks, soak in cool water with a little lemon juice for a short time before washing. For turmeric or curry stains, rub a little liquid detergent into the spot and leave it in gentle shade for an hour before washing. Never use bleach on coloured cotton.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Storage that prevents yellowing and musty smells}}$q$,
    $q$Always store sarees completely dry. Fold them with the border inside, and refold every two or three months along different lines to avoid permanent creases. Wrap each saree in a muslin or soft cotton cloth rather than plastic, which traps moisture. Add neem leaves or a few silica gel packets to the cupboard, especially in monsoon, and air your sarees in shade on a dry day every season.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Special care for handloom and bordered cotton}}$q$,
    $q$Handloom cotton and sarees with contrast borders deserve a gentler routine. Wash them by hand, turn them inside out, and avoid scrubbing the border directly. If two colours sit side by side, rinse quickly in cold water to prevent bleeding, and dry the saree flat or folded over a line with the border in shade. Natural-dyed sarees can release colour for longer, so keep them separate from light shades for the first several washes. Never leave a wet saree folded, as the damp border can stain the body.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Seasonal care routine}}$q$,
    $q$In summer, wash more often and dry quickly in good airflow. In monsoon, iron sarees before folding them away to drive out any hidden moisture, and give your cupboard a dry cloth wipe. In winter, take the sarees out, air them for a few hours in shade, and refold them differently from last time. A small yearly routine like this prevents most of the damage people blame on the fabric.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Quick care checklist}}$q$,
    $q$Wash separately at first, use a mild detergent, rinse until the water runs clear, dry in shade, iron on the reverse while slightly damp, and store completely dry in a breathable cloth. If you remember only these six habits, your cotton sarees will stay fresh and colourful for many years.$q$,
    $q${{h2:Choosing sarees that are easy to care for}}$q$,
    $q$If you want low-maintenance sarees, solid colours are the simplest. Our [Plain Sarees](categoryslug:plain-saree) collection is easy to wash and mix with different blouses, while the full range of [Cotton Sarees for Women](categoryslug:cotton-saree) has options for every budget. For tips on wearing your freshly ironed saree, read our [cotton saree draping guide](blog:how-to-wear-a-cotton-saree).$q$,
    $q${{product:plain-black-cotton-sarees}}$q$
  ]::text[],
  $q$[{"question":"Can I machine wash a cotton saree?","answer":"Yes, on a gentle cycle with a mesh bag and mild detergent. Hand wash handloom sarees and anything with delicate borders."},{"question":"How do I stop my cotton saree colour from running?","answer":"Wash it separately in cold water for the first few washes, avoid long soaking, and dry it in shade away from strong sun."},{"question":"How do I make a cotton saree crisp at home?","answer":"Use rice or arrowroot starch dissolved in cool water, dip the saree for a minute, dry in shade and iron while it is slightly damp."},{"question":"How should I store cotton sarees in monsoon?","answer":"Keep them fully dry, wrap them in muslin, add neem leaves or silica gel to the cupboard, and refold them every few months."}]$q$::jsonb,
